window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"11-01","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"11-01-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-01/","title_he":"א – כיבוד הורים וכבוד שמיים","title_en":"1. Honouring Parents and the Honour of Heaven","body_en":"<p>It is a positive mitzva from the Torah that a person honour his father and his mother, and revere them, as it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother,\" and likewise it is said (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father.\" And the Sages said (Kiddushin 30b): Scripture made the honour of father and mother equal to the honour of the Omnipresent, for it is said (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall <b>revere</b> his mother and his father,\" and it is said (Devarim 10:20): \"You shall <b>revere</b> the Lord your God.\"</p>\n<p>And one must understand why the mitzva of honouring parents occupies so honoured a place, to the point that it was made equal to the honour of Heaven. The explanation is that indeed, a person who does not honour his parents cannot reach faith in Hashem. A person who does not honour his parents is a selfish person, who thinks only of himself, of his pleasures and his private needs; he does not raise his thoughts to those who helped him and did him kindness. Questions such as who looked after him in his childhood and who cared for him in his illness do not interest him; he is occupied with himself alone, and thinks that everything is owed to him, and therefore it does not occur to him that he needs to be grateful to anyone. Such a person will of course not think of the Creator, who is good and does good, who watches over and cares for man at every moment. It does not interest him by whose merit he lives, who brings down the rain, who created the sun and who breathed into him the spirit of life. He is not capable of believing, and is not interested in cleaving to Hashem and walking in His ways and adding goodness and blessing to the world.</p>\n<p>Therefore the Torah made the honour of parents equal to the honour of Heaven, for one who honours his parents prepares his soul for a thought of faith, for a thought of gratitude. He becomes accustomed to thinking about the roots of things, and out of contemplating his parents and recognising the good they have done him, he will go on further to faith in the Creator of the world: \"Lift up your eyes on high, and see who has created these\" (Yeshayahu 40:26).</p>\n<p>Now we can understand a further saying of the Sages: \"There are three partners in a person: the Holy One, blessed be He, his father and his mother. When a person honours his father and his mother, the Holy One, blessed be He, says: I regard them as if I had dwelt among them and they had honoured Me\" (Kiddushin 30b).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-02/","title_he":"ב – יחסי הורים וילדים חוליה בשרשרת הנצח","title_en":"2. The Relations of Parents and Children: A Link in the Chain of Eternity","body_en":"<p>There are two central mitzvot in the Torah regarding parents and regarding children. One is the mitzva of honouring parents, which because of its importance was mentioned in the Ten Commandments. And the second is the mitzva of educating the children, which is in fact a central part of the mitzva of Torah study, about which the Sages said that it is equal to all the mitzvot together (see Yerushalmi Pe'ah 1:1).</p>\n<p>It seems that there is a deep purpose in these mitzvot: to direct a person to connect to the course of the generations, and not to withdraw into himself; to be a person of the collective and not a private person. The mitzva of honouring father and mother connects us to all the generations that have passed. Whereas the second mitzva, educating the children, connects the parents to all the generations that are to come. Through the education they give their children, they will connect to the continuation, to the grandchildren and great-grandchildren and their children until the end of all the generations.</p>\n<p>Through these two mitzvot the Torah directs us to raise our lives to eternity, and to connect through the period of our private lives to all the generations. Through honouring our parents we draw and learn from the generations that have passed all their wisdom and experience. And through the mitzva of educating the children we bequeath and give them all that we have learned, together with our personal contribution. And so, through the short and passing present, we succeed in connecting with eternity, with the generations that have passed and with the generations that will come.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-03/","title_he":"ג – ארבע המצוות הקשורות לכיבוד הורים","title_en":"3. The Four Mitzvot Connected with Honouring Parents","body_en":"<p>Four mitzvot from the Torah deal with the relations of children to their parents, two of them positive mitzvot and two negative. And these are they:<br>1) To honour the parents, as it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother.\" Its meaning is to assist and help them in everything they need.<br>2) To revere them, as it is said (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father.\" Its point is not to harm their honour, not to call them by their names, not to sit on a chair reserved for them, and the like.<br>3) Not to strike one of the parents. Admittedly, there is a general prohibition from the Torah against striking any Jewish person, but the severity of striking parents is far greater, and therefore there is a difference in their punishment. One who strikes his fellow is punished with lashes, and if he injured him, he pays the damages and is not punished with lashes. Whereas one who strikes his father or his mother so that a drop of blood came out is liable to death: if he did so before witnesses who warned him, he is punished by the court with death by strangulation, as it is said (Shemot 21:15): \"And he who strikes his father or his mother shall surely be put to death.\"<br>4) Not to curse one of the parents. Here too, from the Torah it is forbidden to curse any Jew, but one who curses one of his parents transgresses a far more severe prohibition, and this is expressed in his punishment. For one who curses his fellow is punished by the court with lashes, whereas one who curses one of his parents is punished with death by stoning, as it is said (Vayikra 20:9): \"For every man who curses his father or his mother shall surely be put to death; he has cursed his father or his mother; his blood shall be upon him.\" And all this is when two witnesses stood before him and warned him not to curse, and he transgressed and cursed before them while mentioning one of the special names of Hashem. But if he cursed while mentioning an epithet of the Name, such as 'Merciful' or 'Gracious', his law is like one who cursed a Jew, who is punished with lashes. There is a further special stringency regarding one who curses his parents: even if he cursed them after their death, he is punished with death.</p>\n<p>It is an interesting thing that the punishment of one who curses his parents is more severe than the punishment of one who strikes them: although the punishment of both is death, the one who curses is punished with stoning, which is the most severe death, whereas the one who strikes is punished with strangulation, which is the lightest of the four death penalties of the court. The Ramban explains (Shemot 21:15) that for two reasons it is fitting to punish the one who curses with a more severe punishment. <b>a)</b> Because this sin is more common, for fools at the time of their anger tend to rage and curse, and in order to keep them from this the Torah was stricter in their punishment.<br><b>b)</b> It speaks of one who curses while mentioning the Name of Heaven; and if, while mentioning the Name, he harmed the honour of his parents, his sin is most severe. It should be noted that in practice the death penalty of the court is almost never carried out, but is meant to express the severity of the sin (as explained below 6:4).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"All that we said, that the one who curses is punished with stoning and the one who strikes with strangulation, is specifically when he did it before witnesses who warned him not to transgress, and that if he transgressed he would be punished with death, and nevertheless he transgressed. But if he struck one of his parents or cursed him deliberately not before witnesses, in the view of a few poskim his punishment is karet, that is, he will die with his days cut short and his offspring too will be cut off. So it is explained in Yerushalmi Sanhedrin 7:11 regarding one who curses, and so wrote the Ḥinukh 48 regarding one who strikes his father. On the other hand, in the view of most Rishonim there is no punishment of karet for one who strikes or curses deliberately not before witnesses; rather, he is judged in the heavenly court according to the severity of the sin. And so wrote Tosafot, Ketubot 37b s.v. 'mitot', and Tosafot Ha-Rosh there, and the Rambam's Sefer Ha-Mitzvot, negative 352; and the Me'iri, Sanhedrin 65b and Makkot 13b. And so it is explicit in the Torat Kohanim and in the Tosefta at the beginning of Keritot."}]},{"slug":"11-01-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-04/","title_he":"ד – כיבוד ומורא אב ואם","title_en":"4. Honour and Reverence of Father and Mother","body_en":"<p>The proper attitude to parents is made up of two mitzvot: one is reverence, as it is said (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father,\" and the second is honour, as it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother.\" Our Rabbis asked (Kiddushin 31b): What is reverence and what is honour? And they answered: Reverence – he does not stand in his place reserved for him, and does not sit in his place reserved for him, and does not contradict his words. Honour – he assists him in everything he needs: he gives him food and drink, and if necessary he dresses him and covers him, brings him in and takes him out.</p>\n<p>And the question is asked: why in the mitzva of reverence did the Torah put the mother first, saying: \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father,\" whereas in the mitzva of honour the Torah put the father first, saying: \"Honour your father and your mother\"? Why did the Torah vary, once mentioning the mother first and once the father? Rabbi Yehuda Ha-Nasi answered this and said: It is revealed and known before Him who spoke and the world came into being that a son honours his mother more than his father, because she persuades him with words and appeases him; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, put the father first in honour, so that a person should honour his father as he honours his mother. And it is revealed and known before Him who spoke and the world came into being that a son fears his father more than his mother; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, put reverence of the mother before reverence of the father, so that a person should revere his mother as he reveres his father (Kiddushin 30b). Further in the chapter I will sometimes bring examples in the masculine, but as we have learned in the Torah and the words of the Sages, in the mitzva of honouring parents, the father and the mother, the son and the daughter, are equal, except where it is noted otherwise.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-05/","title_he":"ה – ישיבה במקומם וקימה לפניהם","title_en":"5. Sitting in Their Place and Rising Before Them","body_en":"<p>Included in the mitzva of honouring parents is to revere them, that is, to relate to them with respectful awe, as it is said (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father.\" Therefore the sons and daughters must be careful not to sit in the place reserved for the father or the mother. This law applies both to a place reserved for them at the dining table and to a place reserved for them at work or in the synagogue. But in a place that is not reserved for them alone, their children may sit (SA YD 240:2). For example, in many families there are two tables for eating: one in the living room, at which there are fixed places for the parents, where their children may not sit; and the second in the kitchen, around which simple meals are eaten. And since around the kitchen table there are not enough places, there are no fixed places there; and although when the father is present he always eats in a certain place, when he is not present others too are accustomed to sit in that place. Therefore it is not considered a place reserved for the father, and at a time when the father does not need to sit there, the children may sit there.</p>\n<p>There is a further mitzva: to rise in honour of the father or the mother when they enter a room in which the son or daughter is sitting. This law is learned from the mitzva to rise before an elder or a sage, as it is said (Vayikra 19:32): \"You shall rise up before the hoary head, and honour the face of the elder, and you shall fear your God; I am the Lord.\" And each of the parents is regarded by the children as more than an elderly and wise rabbi. Therefore whoever sees one of his parents when he enters the house or leaves it, and likewise when he enters a room in which he is sitting or departs from it, is obligated to stand in his honour until the parent reaches his place or until he goes out.</p>\n<p>Today many children are not accustomed to rise before their parents, and it can be said that since the parents did not educate them in this, it is a sign that they have waived their honour in this matter; and the halakha is that parents who waived their honour – their honour is waived. If so, there is no sin in the children not rising when one of the parents enters. And it is better to ask the parents explicitly whether they waive their honour and agree that their children not rise before them when they come in and go out.</p>\n<p>All this is when the parent enters the house or the room for his own personal need. But if the parent comes to speak with his son or to ask something of him, it is clear that the son is obligated to rise promptly in order to be ready to hear the parent's words and fulfil his request. For it is not respectful that the parent should stand and the son sit; and if the son continues to sit while the parent speaks with him, there is in this an affront to his honour. And even if the father or mother waives it, the waiver does not help where there is an affront to their honour. And likewise before strangers, in a place where it is customary to rise in honour of parents, the children must rise in honour of their parents, for if they do not rise they will appear to be affronting their honour.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The source of the halakha to rise before parents is in Kiddushin 31b, and ibid. 33b. And how many times a day must one rise? Just as they disagreed regarding rising before a rabbi, so they disagreed regarding parents. In the view of the Rif, the Rosh and the SA YD 242:16, one is obligated to rise even a hundred times a day, every time he passes by. Whereas in the view of the Rambam, Tosafot and the Rema, one is obligated to rise only twice a day; but if strangers are present who did not see that he had already risen before him, he must rise before him even several times. And the law is the same regarding rising before parents. Therefore according to the custom of the Sephardim one should rise even a hundred times a day, as the Ḥida wrote in Birkei Yosef 240:21. And the Ashkenazim are accustomed to rise twice a day, and so wrote the Ḥayei Adam, rule 67:7. And if the parents waive their honour, their honour is waived, as explained in SA 240:19. And although the parents waive their honour, if the children nevertheless honour them by rising, they fulfil a mitzva. And where there is disgrace to the parents, even if they waived their honour, in the view of most poskim their honour is not waived; see Birkei Yosef 240:13-14, and Shiyurei Berakha 8, and Sedei Ḥemed, section Kaf, rule 31."}]},{"slug":"11-01-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-06/","title_he":"ו – לא לקרוא בשמם","title_en":"6. Not Calling Them by Their Name","body_en":"<p>Included in reverence of parents is not to call them by their name, in the way their friends call them, but to address them as 'Abba' and 'Imma'. And even not in their presence one may not mention them by their name; rather, their children must always mention them by the title 'Abba' and 'Imma'. And there is a question whether, in addition to the title 'Abba', one may also say his name. According to the custom of those of Sephardi origin, one may say: \"My father Moshe said such-and-such,\" for since he mentioned the title 'my father', it is clear that he honours him, and there is no problem in his also mentioning his name. But according to the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin, he should not say his father's name at all, but only say: \"My father says such-and-such,\" because for the son the father's only title is 'Abba', and if he mentions his name he will somewhat diminish the importance of the concept 'Abba'. But a son who is asked, \"What is your father's name?\" may according to all customs answer and say: \"My father's name is Moshe.\"<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> When a son has a friend whose name is the same as his father's, may he call him by his name?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> In the view of most poskim, if it is a common name, such as Moshe, Yaakov, Raḥel, Esther and the like, then in the presence of the parent whose name is the same as the friend's, one may not call the friend by his name, but should address him in another way. But not in the presence of the parent, one may address him by his ordinary name, because it is clear to all that he means the friend, and there is no affront to the parent in this. And if it is a rare name, such as Kalman, Yifraḥ, Zelda, then even not in the presence of the parent he should not address his friend by his name, but should change it a little.<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>There are communities in which it is customary to name children after the parents while they are still alive, and in those communities there is honour of parents in this, for the parents merit to see in their lifetime the grandson or granddaughter named after them. But one must be careful not to call the children by their full name in the presence of the grandfather or grandmother, but to change their name to a term of endearment and the like. And if the grandfather or grandmother explicitly agrees that the grandchildren be called by their ordinary name even when they are present there, one may call them by their ordinary name, and there is no need to use terms of endearment. But when strangers are present there who will not understand this, and will think that the son is affronting his father's honour, one should address the grandchild by a term of endearment, even though the father waived his honour.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In the view of the Ḥida in his book Yosef Ometz, siman 87, one may say: \"My father Moshe says such-and-such,\" and so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, Shofetim 4. Whereas the view of the Yam Shel Shlomo, Kiddushin 1:65, is that one should not mention the father's name at all, even when one prefixes it with the title 'my father', and so is the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin. And see the book Mora Horim U-Khvodam 6:6 in the note, where he mentioned the various views."},{"n":4,"en":"So is the view of the Rema, YD 240:2, and so it seems that the SA holds there. And so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Shofetim 4. Admittedly, in the view of the Derisha the law is more lenient: if the parent's name is common, even in the presence of the parent one may call the friend by his name; and if the name is rare, in the presence of the parent one may not call the friend, and not in his presence one may. But the prevalent custom follows most poskim, who are stringent in this."}]},{"slug":"11-01-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-07/","title_he":"ז – האיסור להעיר הורים ישנים","title_en":"7. The Prohibition of Waking Sleeping Parents","body_en":"<p>Included in the mitzva of honour and reverence of parents is not to cause them distress. Among the things that may cause a person great distress is when he is woken in the middle of his sleep, and therefore the children must be very careful not to disturb their parents' sleep.</p>\n<p>Well known is the wondrous story of that Roman army commander who ruled in Ashkelon, whose name was Dama ben Netina. Once the Sages of Israel came to him to buy from him precious stones for the breastplate of the ephod, for six hundred thousand gold dinars. But since at that time his father was asleep, and the key to the treasury of stones was lying between his fingers or under his feet, he did not want to wake him from his sleep, and told the Sages that he could not sell to them. The Sages went off to look for suitable stones with other merchants, and Dama ben Netina lost an enormous fortune. The following year the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him his reward, and a red heifer was born in his herd. The Sages of Israel came in to him to buy it. He said to them: I know that if I ask of you all the money in the world you will give it to me; but I ask of you only that money which I lost for the sake of my father's honour (Yerushalmi Pe'ah 1:1; Bavli Kiddushin 31a).</p>\n<p>As for the halakha, the Sefer Ḥasidim (337) wrote that all this is specifically when the son knows that his father does not want to get up from his sleep, even for the sake of a great profit for his son. But if the son knows that his father will be more distressed when he hears that his son lost a great deal of money because he did not want to wake him from his sleep, he may wake him. And nevertheless the Aḥaronim wrote that if possible one should try to find another person to wake the father or the mother, and only when there is no other possibility at all may the son wake his parents. And this of course on condition that it is clear to the son that the parents wish to get up from their sleep in order to help him, and will be distressed if they hear that because of their sleep their son lost something important (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 240:40).</p>\n<p>When the parents themselves ask their son to wake them from their sleep, for example when the father wants to get up from his sleep for Shaḥarit prayer, or asked his son to wake him in order to go out to a wedding, the son may wake his parents without any concern; and on the contrary, he thereby fulfils a mitzva of honouring parents, in that he helps them in what they ask of him.</p>\n<p>When a telephone call comes for one of the parents while he is asleep, and the caller claims that the matter is urgent, the son should not wake his parents. Only if the caller says what the urgency is, and it seems to the son that his parents will indeed be distressed if he does not wake them, may he wake them. And when possible it is preferable to wake them through another person (see the book Mora Horim U-Khvodam 1:29).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-08/","title_he":"ח – כללי מצוות כיבוד","title_en":"8. The Rules of the Mitzva of Honour","body_en":"<p>The essence of the mitzva of honouring parents is to serve them and to assist them in everything they may need. When the children live with their parents, even when the parents are healthy and strong and able to do all the housework themselves, the children are obligated to help their parents. And this is a kal va-ḥomer: for even strange children who are guests in the home of their hosts for a certain period are obligated, out of the obligation to behave with proper conduct, to take part in the burden of the housework; all the more so when it concerns sons and daughters, whom the mitzva of honouring parents obliges to help their parents. For anything that a person is obligated towards his fellow out of the duty of proper conduct, children are obligated towards their parents out of the mitzva of honouring parents. Therefore, whenever the parents ask the child to help in the housework as is customary, and the child refuses, he neglects a mitzva from the Torah of honouring parents.</p>\n<p>And even after the children have left home, if the parents need help, one is obligated to help them. For example, elderly parents who need assistance with eating – it is a mitzva to assist them with their eating. And if they are ill and need help when they get dressed – it is a mitzva to assist them. And likewise elderly parents who need support in walking, when they need to go to the synagogue or to another place – the children must accompany them on their way. And likewise when they need help in cleaning the house – it is a mitzva to help them. And likewise when walking is hard for them and they need assistance with errands, for example when they ask their son to buy food or clothes for them, or to bring them medicines from the pharmacy – it is a mitzva that he carry out their errand.</p>\n<p>And even if assisting them takes a long time, the son must free himself from his other occupations and assist his parents. And even if helping his parents requires him to give up a little of his work or of the time he devotes to his family members – as long as his parents need his help, and helping them does not require him to lose his source of livelihood, he is obligated to free himself from his work and his various occupations in order to assist his parents. And of course, if one of the parents is ill, the sons must free their time and care for him.</p>\n<p>But if the parents do not need real help, but ask their son to come and visit them every day, so that they can enjoy his company or so that he will help them with things they are able to do easily, he has no obligation to visit them every day. For the mitzva of honouring parents requires him to assist them in the things they need, and not to free all his time in order to gladden them. Therefore, if he prefers at that time to learn or to work or to be in the company of his wife and children, he has no obligation to visit them every day. Nevertheless, he is obligated to visit them frequently and to help them from time to time, as is customary in good families; for if not, this will be considered an affront to their honour.</p>\n<p>A son whose parents asked him to buy goods for them in a shop: if the parents have money, he should pay with the parents' money, for the mitzva of honouring parents does not oblige the children to bear financial expenses, but only to help and assist with errands and work. But if the parents have no money to pay for the needs of their food and clothing, or for the upkeep of their home, it is a mitzva for their sons to pay all their needs for them. And if they have no money to pay for their parents, it is a mitzva for them to collect charity for them.</p>\n<p>And if the father decided not to work and expects his children to support him, or he wants to save most of his money and live a life of want, in this case the children need not see to all his needs. For only in a situation of no choice is there a mitzva on the children to help their parents with their money, and when necessary even to collect charity for them; but when the parents cause themselves to live a life of want, the children are not obligated to help them.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The essence of the mitzva of honouring parents is to serve them, that is, to assist them in their bodily needs at a time when they need it – to feed them, to dress them and the like. Therefore, if they ask that he come to visit when they have no real need, there is no obligation to fulfil their wish. Of course, if he visits, he fulfils a mitzva, but there is no obligation in this from the standpoint of honouring parents. But if he distances himself from them and does not come to visit and help them as it is customary for sons to visit their parents and help them, this will be considered an affront to their honour, and he will transgress the mitzva of reverence of parents. And see SA YD 240:2-4. Regarding spending money on honouring parents: in Kiddushin 31b they disagreed whether the mitzva of honour is at the expense of the son's money or the parents'. And the Rif, the Rambam, the Rosh and Tosafot ruled that it is at the parents' expense. But all this is when the parents have money to finance their needs; but when they do not, it is a mitzva for the children to support their parents. And if the sons have a small sum, sufficient for their own needs or for their parents' needs, they are not obligated to give their money to the parents, as explained in SA 240:5. But the Aḥaronim wrote that it is a mitzva for the sons to go from door to door in order to collect charity for their parents' needs, and so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Shofetim 8. And this is not an obligation, because the mitzva of honouring parents obliges one to assist them with one's actual body, such as dressing, feeding and the like, but going from door to door is only a preparation for the honour itself, and in this they are not obligated; and so it is explained in Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 240:22, and so is the view of the Baḥ and the Shakh. And according to the Ḥazon Ish (Kiddushin, siman 148, p. 32) it is a complete obligation on them to go from door to door for poor parents. And if the parents are able to support themselves properly but do not want to work, or they save a great deal, the children are not obligated to support them; and so it is implied by the Ḥatam Sofer YD 229, and so it is brought in the name of Rabbi Abba Shaul in the book Mora Horim U-Khvodam 1:19.<br>In a situation of conflict, that is, when the parents really need help, but if the son assists his parents in what they need he will lose his livelihood: if as a result he will have nothing to eat that day and will need to go from door to door, it is ruled in SA 240:5 that his livelihood comes first. And likewise the Aḥaronim ruled that if by assisting his parents he will be absent a great deal from his work, to the point that he will lose his job and will need to go from door to door, in this case his livelihood comes first and he is exempt from honouring parents. See the Rishon Le-Tziyon, and the Be'ur Ha-Gra 14, and Shevet Ha-Levi part 2, 111:3."}]},{"slug":"11-01-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-09/","title_he":"ט – חובת הבן והבת","title_en":"9. The Obligation of the Son and the Daughter","body_en":"<p>Sometimes one hears sons complaining against the institutions of the state that they do not help their elderly parents enough: the allowance is small, there are not enough nursing hospitals, and the like. But they must remember that the mitzva of honouring parents is placed upon the sons. And even when the State of Israel is rich and can provide every elderly person with his needs, the central role will still belong to the children; for what are the cold laws that a state can enact, compared with the warm and familial relationship that the children give their parents?</p>\n<p>The Sages said: it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother,\" and immediately afterwards it is said (ibid. 20:13): \"You shall not murder.\" Why were these mitzvot placed next to each other? To teach us that if a person has food in his house and does not let his father and mother benefit from it, it is as if he is a murderer all his days before the Omnipresent. Therefore it is said \"honour\" and after it \"you shall not murder\" (Yalkut Shimoni, Va'etḥanan 830).</p>\n<p>And so it was ruled as halakha (SA YD 240:5) that if the son has the means to support his needy parents, he is obligated to support them, even if as a result he will need to lower his standard of living. And when the court has the power, if it sees a son who can support his parents and does not support them, it must compel him to support his parents (see the next halakha and the note). And although by law a person may give his charity money to his parents, if the son has the means to support his parents and to continue to give a tithe of his money to charity, it is not fitting that he give the charity money to his parents. And the Sages said that a curse will come upon one who supports his parents from the money of his tithe, because it is not fitting to support parents with charity money (Rema YD 240:5).</p>\n<p>The mitzva of honouring parents belongs to the son and the daughter equally, but all this is when they are not married. But after the wedding a difference is created between them: the son is commanded to continue to honour his parents as he honoured them before the wedding, but the married daughter is not obligated in the mitzva of honouring parents as she was obligated while she was single. It is clear that she must relate to her parents with honour and reverence, but she is not obligated to help and assist them as she was obligated before the wedding. For in marriage she undertook to care first for the needs of her home and the raising of her small children, and she is not able to help her parents as she was while single. And there is even a concern that if she is preoccupied with honouring parents, the proper relations that must prevail between her and her husband will be harmed because of it. Therefore the Torah exempted her from the mitzva of helping her parents in everything they may need (SA YD 240:17, 24). But when there is no conflict between honouring her parents and the peace of her home and her duty of caring for her children, the mitzva from the Torah to assist the parents returns to her (Shakh YD 240:19). Today, with the rise in life expectancy, at the stage at which the parents need help, their daughters have usually already finished raising their small children, and they can help them without harming the peace of their home; and consequently they again become obligated in the mitzva of honouring parents from the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-10/","title_he":"י – האם אפשר לכוף את הבנים לכבד את הוריהם","title_en":"10. Can the Sons Be Compelled to Honour Their Parents?","body_en":"<p>Sometimes a question arises in the laws of honouring parents: what is the law when there is a rich son whose parents are poor and have no money to live on, but their rich son estranges himself from them with all kinds of claims and pretexts – according to halakha, can the court intervene and compel the son to support them or not? And likewise, when one of the parents is ill and needs assistance, can the son be compelled to care for him?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The basic rule is that any mitzva whose reward is mentioned in the Torah, the court does not compel its fulfilment (Ḥullin 110b). For so it is implied from the Torah: the reward mentioned beside it is what should spur its fulfilment, and not any compulsion of the court. And since regarding the mitzva of honouring parents its reward is mentioned beside it, as it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother, that your days may be long,\" its fulfilment is not compelled. And if a person does not want to help his sick parents, the court does not compel him to help them. And likewise, if the parents are poor, from the standpoint of the mitzva of honouring parents the court cannot compel him to provide his parents with all their needs.</p>\n<p>But from the standpoint of the mitzva of charity, the son can be compelled to give his parents money that will suffice for their subsistence. For according to halakha the charity collectors can oblige the rich to give charity for the support of the poor. And since the parents are the closest relatives, consequently, according to the order of precedence in the mitzva of charity, the charity collectors can compel the son to give his parents all the money that he is obligated to give to charity, which is at the least a tenth of his profits; and if necessary he can be obliged to give them even a fifth of his profits (Beit Yosef and Rema YD 240:5).<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>All this is in a corrupt situation in which the son does not want to support his parents and must be obliged to do so by the law of the mitzva of charity. But it is fitting that the son help his parents with all his ability, and give them all their needs, while the charity money that he must give he should give for the needs of other poor people. And we have already learned (in the previous halakha) that the Sages said (Kiddushin 32a) that a person who is able to support his parents and in addition to give a tithe of his money to charity – a curse will come upon him if he supports his parents from the tithe money intended for charity (Rema YD 240:5).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"It is difficult, for in the mitzva of charity too its reward is stated beside it, as it is said (Devarim 15:10): \"You shall surely give him… because for this thing the Lord your God will bless you in all your work and in all that you put your hand to.\" So how can one compel its fulfilment? But since regarding charity a negative commandment is also stated (ibid. 15:7): \"You shall not harden your heart, nor shut your hand from your needy brother,\" one can compel the fulfilment of the mitzva of charity (Ri in Tosafot, Bava Batra 8b). Some hold that the court can compel the son to give his parents even more than what he is obligated to give to charity, and so is the view of the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan YD 240:20."}]},{"slug":"11-01-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-11/","title_he":"יא – מצווה לגור בקרבת ההורים","title_en":"11. A Mitzva to Live Near the Parents","body_en":"<p>Included in the mitzva of honouring parents is a mitzva that a person live near his parents' place of residence, so that he can honour them and assist them in everything they may need. But if he lives in a distant place, they will not be able to turn to him with a request for help, and it turns out that he has kept himself from the mitzva of honouring parents.</p>\n<p>And so we find that the Sages said of Avraham Avinu that he was hesitant to leave Ḥaran and abandon Teraḥ his father in his old age, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him (Bereshit 12:1): \"Go forth from your land, and from your birthplace, and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you,\" and thereby exempted him from the mitzva of honouring parents (Bereshit Rabba 39:8). And likewise the Sages said that Yaakov Avinu was punished in that Yosef his son was lost to him for twenty-two years, because he himself left his parents and went to Ḥaran for twenty-two years. And although Yaakov was forced to flee from Esav his brother, who wanted to kill him, and did so at his parents' command, since in practice he did not honour his parents in those years, and did not even try to return quickly when he could, he was punished for it. But for the sake of Torah study a person may leave his parents' home. And likewise Yaakov Avinu went to the study house of Shem and Ever and learned there fourteen years, and for this he was not punished (Megilla 17a).</p>\n<p>Therefore every person should try to live near his parents' place of residence, so that he can honour them and assist them as needed. And if he wants to live in a distant place, he must ask permission from his parents. And even if he wants to move to a distant place for the sake of a mitzva, he must receive permission from them. But for the sake of Torah study he may move to another city without receiving their permission. And likewise he may move to a place distant from his parents' home in order to give his children a better Torah education without receiving their permission. And likewise for a vital need of livelihood, the son may live at a distance without asking their permission (based on Arukh Ha-Shulḥan YD 240:36).</p>\n<p>Today, many times the apartments in the area where the parents live are expensive, and this too is a justified reason to live in another place. And even if the son is able to buy a small apartment near them, if as a result he will not be able to establish a family blessed with children as he wishes, it is preferable that he buy an apartment in a cheap place and establish a family blessed with children.</p>\n<p>And likewise one who lives with his parents abroad – it is a mitzva that he make aliya to the Land, even against their will (Maharam of Rothenburg, gate 2, 28, 79; Mabit part 1, 139).</p>\n<p>Elderly parents who need close care and wish to live with their son – it is a mitzva for the son to house his parents in his home and thereby fulfil the mitzva of honouring parents at every moment. And all this on condition that there is room in his home and his wife agrees. But if she does not agree, or the place is cramped and it is clear that as a result of the parents living in their home great difficulties and complications will arise, he may not bring his parents into his home without complete permission from his wife. And he must arrange an old-age home for them, or come to their home every day and assist them. And even if he has to sleep in their home many nights, he should sleep there and not bring them into his home without the complete consent of his wife. And likewise, when the wife's parents need close care, it is a mitzva for her to house them in her home and to fulfil the mitzva of honouring parents towards them at every moment. But if her husband does not agree, the wife cannot compel him to house them in their home, and she must help them in their own home as far as she is able, without the needs of their small children and her home being significantly harmed.</p>\n<p>A bride and groom who are about to marry, and the bride wants to live in the north, whereas the groom's parents demand of their son that he live near their home in the south. And the state of affairs is that if the groom listens to his parents, the bride will break off her connection with him. The groom may decide to live far from his parents so that he can marry her, for the mitzva of marriage comes first. But even when he lives in the north, his duty will be to continue to visit his parents from time to time, as is customary among good sons. And if they need real help, he will be obligated to travel to them more and help them according to their need, even if his livelihood is harmed as a result, provided that he does not endanger his source of livelihood.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"For the sake of educating the children one may move away from the parents' place of residence, for their education is equal to Torah study, and for the sake of Torah study one may leave the parents' place. And likewise for the sake of livelihood it is permitted, for we have already learned that honouring parents need not be at the expense of the son's livelihood (SA YD 240:5).<br>A husband has no right to bring one of his parents into his home without his wife's consent, because at the time of their marriage he undertook in the ketuba to treat her properly, and she is a partner in the home, and it is not possible to do something without her consent. But it is his duty to care for them, even if he has to leave his home for a number of days or weeks. And so ruled Rabbi Elyashiv and Rabbi Abba Shaul, as brought in the book Mora Horim U-Khvodam 1:41. There is no need to cancel a match because of his parents' demand that he live near them, as we learned in a similar matter in the Rema 240:25 in the name of the Mahari'k.<br>As we learned in halakha 9, after marriage the obligation of honouring parents applies to the husband towards his parents, whereas the wife is exempt from honouring her parents, because of the conflict between her duty towards her new family and her duty towards her parents, as explained in SA YD 240:17. But if the husband does not object, or the children have grown up and in practice there is no conflict, the mitzva of honouring her parents returns to its place. Therefore, if the wife's parents need their daughter to house them in her home, and the husband agrees, it is a mitzva to bring them into their home and care for them in everything they need."}]},{"slug":"11-01-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-12/","title_he":"יב – יחס של כבוד","title_en":"12. A Respectful Attitude","body_en":"<p>In order to fulfil the mitzva of honouring father and mother properly, it is important to pay attention to the way in which one fulfils the mitzva. It happens that elderly parents have nothing to live on and need help; and as we have learned, the responsibility of supporting them with dignity is placed upon their children, and no less important is that the assistance be given with a pleasant countenance. For there is a son who helps his parents with a scowling face and with displeasure. And the parents have no choice; they need his help, they have no one else to help them. But their hearts ache within them over their son whom they loved so much, and who now in their old age treats them with contempt and lack of compassion. And about this the saying goes: \"One father supports ten sons and is glad, and ten sons cannot support one father.\"</p>\n<p>And likewise the Sages said: there is one who feeds his father fattened chickens and inherits Gehinnom, and there is one who makes him grind at the mill and inherits the Garden of Eden. How does one feed his father fattened chickens and inherit Gehinnom? It once happened that a man used to feed his father fattened chickens. Once his father said to him: \"My son, where do these chickens come from?\" He said to him: \"Old man, old man, eat and be silent, for the dogs eat and are silent.\" It turns out that this one feeds his father fattened chickens and inherits Gehinnom.</p>\n<p>How does one make him grind at the mill and inherit the Garden of Eden? It once happened that a man was grinding at the mill. The king sent to bring millers for his work, and commanded his servants to search for people who were not working, so that they would grind for him. The son said to his father: \"Father, go in and grind in my place, and I will go to the king's work; for if it comes to disgrace, I will be disgraced and not you, and if to lashes, I will be lashed and not you.\" It turns out that this one makes his father grind at the mill and inherits the Garden of Eden (Yerushalmi Pe'ah 1:1; Kiddushin 31b in Rashi).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-13/","title_he":"יג – עד היכן כיבוד הורים","title_en":"13. How Far Does Honouring Parents Go?","body_en":"<p>Question: Must the son in every case be silent and not hurt his parents, or are there cases in which the parents shame the son so much that he may pay them back in kind and hurt them?</p>\n<p>Answer: So it was ruled as halakha: if the son was dressed in fine clothes and sitting at the head of the congregation, and his father and mother came and tore his clothes, and struck him on his head, and spat in his face – he should not shame them, but should be silent and fear the King of the kings of kings, who commanded him to revere them (SA YD 240:3). And in general one must know that when the father or the mother are ordinary people, there would seemingly be no need to command this, since every decent person understands that their children must honour them. The main novelty of the mitzva is that even when the parents are not worthy of honour, it is a mitzva to honour them. And so wrote Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi (Tokhaḥat Ḥayim on parashat Toledot), that the main command of the Torah is directed towards troublesome parents whose traits are poor.</p>\n<p>Even if the parents took a purse of gold coins of his and threw it before him into the sea, because of their anger or their madness, he should not shame them and should not be angry against them, but should accept the decree of Scripture and be silent (SA YD 240:8).</p>\n<p>But financially the son need not bear the damage that the parents caused him. Therefore he can sue them in the court, and of course they will be obligated to pay him. And if he can stop them by force from throwing his wallet, some say that it is preferable that he stop them by force, on condition that he does not hurt them but only restrains them. And some say that since he can sue them afterwards in court, he should not stop them by force. Only when it is clear to the son that afterwards the parents themselves will be distressed and will complain against him, why did you not stop us by force, may the son stop them, since this is in fact their wish (Rema YD 240:8).</p>\n<p>And it is so hard to fulfil the mitzva of honouring father and mother in all its details and fine points that one of the great Amora'im of the Land of Israel said a sharp and shocking statement. Rabbi Yoḥanan said: happy is one who did not see his father and his mother, because he certainly did not neglect the mitzva of honouring father and mother. Indeed, Rabbi Yoḥanan himself was an orphan from the hour of his birth: when his mother conceived him his father died, and when she gave birth to him his mother died. Admittedly, it is clear that the best thing is that a person have parents and honour them properly. And happy is one who merited to honour his parents, and the orphan loses this important mitzva. But Rabbi Yoḥanan found a positive side in orphanhood: that the orphan is saved from stumbling in the mitzva of honouring parents.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-14/","title_he":"יד – מי שנטרפה דעת הוריו","title_en":"14. One Whose Parents' Minds Have Become Disordered","body_en":"<p>One of the sad questions regarding honouring parents is what a person should do whose parents' minds have become disordered: how should he behave with them?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> Even though their minds have become disordered, he should not disgrace them, and he should try to behave with them with love and respect according to their understanding, until Heaven has mercy on them. And if they have become very deranged, to the point that he is no longer able to see them in such a state, and every time he meets them he comes to anger and quarrelling – if the parents can manage on their own, it is preferable that he leave them and move to another place, and ask others to treat them properly.</p>\n<p>An example of this is brought in the Talmud (Kiddushin 31b). Rabbi Assi had an elderly mother whose mind had become disordered. She asked him to buy her jewellery, and he bought it. Afterwards she wanted to marry, and at every moment she would urge him to see to a match for her. But since it was not possible to find a groom for an elderly woman with a disordered mind like hers, Rabbi Assi would always answer her that he would try to look into the matter. But when his mother began to demand of him that he find her a groom exactly like him, young, talented and handsome, Rabbi Assi could not bear it, and left her place.</p>\n<p>And all this, of course, on condition that the parents can look after themselves, that is, they are able to provide themselves with food and clothing and all that is necessary for their subsistence, only their minds are disordered, and every time they see the son they demand of him strange things that the son's mind cannot bear. But if the parents have become so weak, in their minds or in their bodies, that they cannot subsist by their own powers, the son may not leave them. Rather, if his financial situation allows, he should hire another person for them to care for them, or arrange a place for them in a nursing institution. But if there is no institution that can accept them, and he also has no financial means to hire a carer for them, he must care for his parents, while making the utmost effort to behave with them pleasantly and calmly (SA YD 240:10; Taz 14; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 32).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-15/","title_he":"טו – יחס ההורים לילדים ושלא להכות","title_en":"15. The Parents' Attitude to the Children, and Not Striking","body_en":"<p>Although the mitzva of honouring parents is exceedingly important, the Shulḥan Arukh wrote (YD 240:19): \"A person is forbidden to make his yoke heavy upon his children and to be exacting about his honour with them, so that he not bring them to a stumbling block; rather, he should forgive and turn a blind eye to them, for a father who waived his honour – his honour is waived.\"</p>\n<p>Perhaps by law the parents can demand of their children that they honour them more, but this will not help. No benefit will come from the parents always complaining about their children that they do not honour them enough. On the contrary, children like to honour their parents willingly and out of love, not by compulsion. Consideration for the children's wishes and their dignity will only contribute to the children honouring their parents more.</p>\n<p>Moreover, a father who waived his honour – his honour is waived. That is, in a case where the parents let the son off from taking such trouble to honour them, the son is exempt. Of course, even when the parents waive it, if the son honours them he fulfils a mitzva; but if he does not honour them, he will have no transgression. And do the parents want their son to be punished for the sin of neglecting the mitzva of honouring parents?!</p>\n<p>The most successful way of educating the children to honour their parents is when they see a personal example from them, how they themselves honour their own parents. And one who was negligent in this when his parents were alive should not be surprised that his children do not honour him, and that when he needs help his children do not help him; they learned it from him.</p>\n<p>In the past, in a case where a father would strike his grown son, even if there was a certain justice in his deed, the court would punish the father and excommunicate him, because he thereby transgressed the prohibition (Vayikra 19:14): \"Nor put a stumbling block before the blind,\" for certainly his son will resent him and bear a grudge against him in his heart, and perhaps even strike him back (SA YD 240:20; see above halakha 3, and in the next halakha, on the severity of the prohibition of striking one's father).</p>\n<p>Although in the past good parents would educate their children also with the help of blows, and it is even said (Mishlei 13:24): \"He who spares his rod hates his son,\" and the Sages said (Shemot Rabba 1:1): \"Whoever withholds discipline from his son, in the end he comes to bad ways and hates him\" – today one should minimise corporal punishment.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Until a generation or two ago, decent parents were accustomed to give their children vigorous blows for every failure and sin, in order to educate them and imprint good and upright traits in them. So they raised generations of upright and good Jews. But when the social reality changes, and a parent who strikes with a belt or with vigorous blows is regarded as a criminal, one should minimise corporal punishment. And even if in the parents' view they acted with moderation and out of good intentions to educate the child, the child may hear from his friends and teachers that he needs psychological treatment because he is the victim of severe abuse from a violent and dangerous father. Following such words the child will really need treatment, because the social reaction will create in the child's soul a reality much stronger than the blows themselves that he received. Therefore the parents must adapt the educational means to the social norms, and everything according to the custom of the country and the place. And for those children who received hard blows from their parents, it is recommended to overcome public opinion, and to know that their parents acted in a way that was considered completely reasonable at another time, even though today their way is not accepted. This knowledge can at once ease for them the feeling of insult and injury, and help them to honour their parents as the Torah commands."}]},{"slug":"11-01-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-16/","title_he":"טז – הורים רשעים","title_en":"16. Wicked Parents","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> Does the mitzva of honour and reverence of parents apply to a wicked father who is a habitual sinner, or perhaps, since the father has so corrupted his traits, the son is exempt from the obligation to honour him?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The poskim disagreed about this (SA YD 240:18). In the view of the author of the Shulḥan Arukh, Rabbi Yosef Karo, even if his father was wicked and a habitual sinner, he is obligated to honour him and revere him. And not only this, but even if the son is a mamzer, that is, his father begot him in a forbidden union, and thereby blemished the son and caused that all his days he will not be able to marry a Jewish woman – even in such a case the son is obligated to honour his wicked father, that is, to honour him and assist him when he needs help.</p>\n<p>By contrast, in the view of Rabbi Moshe Isserles (the Rema), the author of the glosses on the Shulḥan Arukh, there is no mitzva for the son to honour his wicked father; for all that the Torah commanded to honour parents is specifically ordinary parents who are not wicked, but the Torah did not command to honour the wicked. And it should be emphasised that a wicked father is specifically a father who cleaves to evil and sins a great deal, knowingly harms other people or commits transgressions out of spite. But an ordinary person who sometimes stumbles in a transgression – it is clear that his son is commanded to honour him and revere him. And even when all agree that the father is wicked, and in the view of the Rema there is no mitzva to honour and revere him, nevertheless the son may not insult him or cause him distress.</p>\n<p>Sometimes ba'alei teshuva err and think that they need not honour their parents since they are secular. But the halakha is that in our time, every person who behaves according to the accepted ways of proper conduct and morality, even though he does not keep the mitzvot, is not considered wicked, and certainly his son is obligated to honour him and revere him. For in this time most people who do not keep the mitzvot do not do so out of an evil wish to spite, but because of the general state of the generation. Therefore a ba'al teshuva is obligated to honour his parents even though they do not keep the mitzvot.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-17/","title_he":"יז – לדון את ההורים לכף זכות","title_en":"17. Judging the Parents Favourably","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> Does the mitzva of honouring parents also oblige one to esteem the parents, or is one who despises them in his heart, as long as he helps them in what they need and treats them politely, also considered as fulfilling the mitzva?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The mitzva of honouring parents must be fulfilled in deed and in thought, as the Sefer Ḥaredim wrote: one must honour the parents in the heart; and one who honours his parents in deeds but they are despicable in his eyes – about him it is said (Devarim 27:16): \"Cursed be he who dishonours his father or his mother.\" The meaning of 'dishonours' is despises. And about this Shlomo said (Mishlei 30:17): \"The eye that mocks at a father and scorns to obey a mother, the ravens of the valley shall pick it out, and the young eagles shall eat it.\" And it once happened that a certain man supported his mother, only she was despised in his eyes because she married another man after his father's death, and he was punished in that he died and the ravens pecked out his eyes (Sefer Ḥaredim, positive mitzvot that depend on the heart, 35).<sup>9</sup></p>\n<p>And likewise it is written in the book Ḥayei Adam (67:3): \"Honour is in thought and in deed and speech… that is, he should imagine in his eyes that they are great and honoured people of the land, even though in the eyes of other people they are not important at all. And this is the essence of honour; for if not, it is written (Yeshayahu 29:13): With their mouth and with their lips they honour Me, but their heart is far from Me.\"</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> One who thinks that his parents are angry and irritable, or greedy for money, or inclined to lie, or unfair – how can he honour them in his thoughts? Is it a mitzva for him to deceive himself, to ignore their bad qualities and to fix in his mind that they are truly righteous and upright?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a mitzva to judge every person favourably (Shevu'ot 30a), as it is said (Vayikra 19:15): \"In righteousness shall you judge your fellow,\" and as the Sages said (Avot 1:6): \"Judge every person favourably.\" That is, to see the good in him, and to know that although there is also bad in him, the good in him is the main thing. And even when a certain incident can be interpreted for good or for bad, it is a mitzva to interpret it for good. And in this merit he too will be judged favourably in Heaven: since he emphasises the good and sees it as the main thing, when he is judged too they will see mainly the good in him (Shabbat 127b). And if so towards every Jew, all the more so must a person judge his parents favourably, since the mitzva of honouring parents too requires it.</p>\n<p>And the fulfilment of this mitzva greatly benefits the son. For a person's parents are his roots, and when he sees good things in them, he connects to his good roots, and by its power he strengthens his good powers and continues all his life in goodness. And if he sees the bad in them, he strengthens also the bad within himself, and consequently continues his life in bad.</p>\n<p>Admittedly, when it is clear that there is sin in the parents, the son may not ignore it; and if the parents tell him to commit a transgression, he should not listen to them (below, halakha 20). And despite this, he must judge them favourably: that the flaws in their traits and their deeds are because they did not learn enough, or because of their emotional or physical difficulties, and perhaps the trials they are coping with are unbearably hard.</p>\n<p>And on the other hand, he should contemplate the positive sides in the parents. For in every person there is a special good trait in which there is no one in the world like him; for if there were another person like him, the Holy One, blessed be He, would not have created him for nothing. And in that thing in which he is unique, he is considered truly the greatest of the generation, upon whom the whole world stands. And every person should contemplate and delve into all the good sides of his father and his mother, and thereby he will be able to honour them all the more.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"So wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, Shofetim 23, and Yalkut Yosef part 1, pp. 103-104. Admittedly, from the view of the Rambam (Laws of Rebels 5:15) it is implied that only one who disgraces them in words or by hinting transgresses the prohibition of \"Cursed be he who dishonours his father or his mother,\" and so wrote the Radbaz there. But it is clear that according to them too a person may not disgrace his parents in his heart, because this disgrace will bring him against his will to disgracing them in deed."}]},{"slug":"11-01-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-18/","title_he":"יח – טיפול פסיכולוגי שפוגע בכיבוד הורים","title_en":"18. Psychological Treatment That Harms Honouring Parents","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> There are psychologists who tend to blame all the patient's problems on his parents, who pressured him, were angry with him and perhaps even struck him. And since the parents are to blame for all the troubles and problems that he causes himself and his friends, the patient can free himself of his burdens. Sometimes the psychologist advises hurling these accusations at the parents in order to unload the burden weighing on the patient's heart. Is such psychological treatment permitted?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> If the treatment will cause the relations of honour and reverence between the son and his parents to deteriorate, this treatment is forbidden, since it harms the mitzva of 'honouring parents'. And even if we were certain that this treatment is psychologically helpful, one may not take part in it. And just as a person may not steal in order to ease his suffering, so he may not transgress the mitzva of 'honouring parents' in order to ease his suffering. Moreover, we believe that all the mitzvot were given for our good, and so it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour your father and your mother, that your days may be long upon the land which the Lord your God gives you.\" Therefore, even if this treatment helps for a certain time, in the end his distress will increase, and the void that opened in his heart will grow larger. He will make himself an orphan in the most terrible sense – an 'orphanhood of the soul'. An orphan has at least a figure to whom he can cling in his memory, whereas this one will not have even a figure to cling to. Every person needs roots. Willy-nilly he is connected to his parents. Even if he wants to, he will not succeed in forgetting his parents and cutting himself off from them. Even if he wraps himself in anger towards them, within his heart he will know that he caused them terrible distress, that he brought down their old age in sorrow, and he too will die in the end lonely and sad.</p>\n<p>But treatment that teaches one to relate to the parents properly, with honour and reverence, and at the same time to recognise their weaknesses out of an attitude of esteem and compassion, is permitted and desirable. For there is no mitzva that the children should think that everything their parents do and think is perfect and holy. On the contrary, the children must learn to examine reality honestly in order to choose the good. If not, they will not be able to fulfil the halakha that establishes that if the parents ask their son to commit a transgression, he may not listen to them (SA YD 240:15; below 20).</p>\n<p>Similarly, the Sha'arei Teshuva (1:40) wrote that a person must confess also the sin of his fathers, as it is said (Vayikra 26:40): \"And they shall confess their iniquity and the iniquity of their fathers.\" For children tend to continue the customs of their parents; for example, if their father is wasteful or angry, they may be like him. And only by confessing and resolving in their heart not to behave like their father can they open a new page. And if they do not confess, they are guilty of continuing the same bad deeds that their parents did.</p>\n<p>Therefore, even one who thinks that his parents erred in his education, because they were angry with him and struck him, or gave in to him and accustomed him to be lazy, must judge them favourably: to understand the pressures that weighed on them, and to resolve in his heart not to repeat their mistakes, and to contemplate with esteem how, despite the difficulties, they succeeded in doing beautiful and good things in their lives. To try to see how everything was for the good (Berakhot 60b), and that even the things that seemed bad in his eyes developed him after the fact, made him sensitive, toughened, deep, and paved his way for good. And further, he should contemplate much the good sides of his parents, for thereby he will be able to esteem and honour them properly. In this way he will be able to connect to the good he received from his parents and continue to develop himself in a complete way (see note 8 on the attitude towards parents who strike).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-19/","title_he":"יט – הורים מתעללים","title_en":"19. Abusive Parents","body_en":"<p>However, one must distinguish between parents who are excessively angry and whose education is overly strict, but whose basic intention is positive – whom one is obligated to honour and to judge favourably – and a parent who is sadistically cruel towards his children. Sadistic behaviour is physically and emotionally dangerous, and therefore it must be stopped. And the child who suffers from sadistic abuse must be strengthened and encouraged, and told that the blows he received are not his fault. All the more so when it concerns forbidden sexual relations. And of course these children must be saved from the dangers that threaten them. (One must be careful to distinguish between disgraceful and ugly behaviour and forbidden sexual relations; there are those who err in this and create severe problems.)</p>\n<p>Yet even in such a terrible and complicated reality, one should try to find a way to judge the wicked parent favourably: to explain to the child that although the parent's deeds are utterly forbidden, and on no account may they be repeated, nevertheless the wicked parent is apparently sick with a severe illness, physical or mental, and therefore he sinned. The more the child understands that the parent who abused him is sick, the more he will be able to separate between the terrible deeds he did and those moments of kindness in which he behaved like an ordinary parent. To those moments he will be able to cling all his days; they can be the positive foundation by whose power he will be able to build his good relations with the children he will have in the future.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-20","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-20/","title_he":"כ – כיבוד הורים ושמירת מצוות","title_en":"20. Honouring Parents and Keeping the Mitzvot","body_en":"<p>It is said in the Torah (Vayikra 19:3): \"Every man shall revere his mother and his father, and you shall keep My Sabbaths.\" From here: if his parents said to him, desecrate the Sabbath, he should not listen to his parents and should not desecrate the Sabbath. For both the son and the parents are commanded in the honour of the Omnipresent (Yevamot 5b). And from this the Sages learned (Bava Metzia 32a) that regarding the other mitzvot of the Torah too, if the parents told the son to transgress one of them, even a mitzva from the words of the Sages, he may not listen to them, since they themselves are forbidden to say so, for they too are commanded in the honour of the Omnipresent (SA YD 240:15).</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes it happens that the parents quarrel with a certain person, a neighbour or a relative, and the dispute grows deeper, from small insults to the breaking of ties, until the parents demand that their son too break off the ties he had with that person. The son, on the other hand, does not want to take part in his parents' quarrel, and in his view one should maintain good relations with every person. The parents for their part enlist the mitzva of honouring parents on their side, and accuse the son of betrayal, and of abandoning his parents for the sake of some wretched aunt or wicked neighbour… And the question is what the son should do in such a case.</p>\n<p>The Shulḥan Arukh ruled (YD 240:16): a father who commanded his son not to speak with so-and-so, and the son wants to be reconciled with him immediately – he need not take his father's view into account. For the Torah forbade hating or behaving with hatred towards a neighbour or relative; rather, one should relate to all with love, as it is said (Vayikra 19:17-18): \"You shall not hate your brother in your heart… and you shall love your neighbour as yourself; I am the Lord.\" Therefore the son may not listen to his parents.</p>\n<p>Similarly, if the parents object to their children going to the wedding of one of their relatives who is in dispute with them, the children may not listen to them, and they must fulfil the mitzva of gladdening the bride and groom against the parents' will. And when there is a doubt whether perhaps there is justice in the parents' claim, one should ask a Torah scholar.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> How should a son behave whose father asks him to buy him a packet of cigarettes? On the one hand we have learned (Peninei Halakha, Likkutim II 9:8) that there is a prohibition from the Torah to smoke cigarettes, because it is harmful to health, and it is forbidden to assist in a transgression; and on the other hand there is a mitzva to honour father and mother.</p>\n<p>Rabbi Ḥayim David Ha-Levi answered that since it has been ruled that honouring parents does not override the mitzvot of the Torah, the son may not comply with the father's request and buy him cigarettes. Admittedly, in the view of most poskim there is no Torah prohibition here of \"Nor put a stumbling block before the blind\" (Vayikra 19:14), because the Torah prohibition applies only when without the assistance the transgression would not have been carried out, and here even without the son the father can obtain cigarettes. Nevertheless, it is forbidden by the words of the Sages to assist in a transgression, and therefore the son may not buy cigarettes for his father (Aseh Lekha Rav 6:58).</p>\n<p>In principle this is indeed the strict law; but if the matter will cause a great quarrel, and serious harm to the relations of love and respect that should exist between him and his father, because refraining from buying the cigarettes will not be understood by the father, it is preferable that the son buy his father cigarettes.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"However, when the parents command the son not to speak with so-and-so, he should not listen to them even when the matter will cause a great quarrel. Ideally, one should try to make a change for the sake of peace in order to minimise the harm, but in practice he should not listen to the parents. But in the matter of the cigarettes the prohibition is in causing another to sin, and consequently what benefit will be caused if he refrains from helping him in a light transgression and causes him to sin in a severe transgression? Similarly, Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach wrote in Minḥat Shlomo part 1, 35:1, that one should not refrain from offering food to one who will not bless over it, because not giving the offering will cause a more severe transgression of hatred than the transgression that will be caused if he gives him food and he does not bless. Thus far.<br>But it seems that if the smoking would cause an imminent danger to life, it would be forbidden to give it to him, as they wrote in Beit Leḥem Yehuda YD 240:15: \"A father who was ill, and the doctors told him not to drink water and not to eat such-and-such a thing, and the father asked the son to give him water or that thing which the doctors told him not to eat, and said: if you do not give it to me I will not forgive you, neither in this world nor in the World to Come – he is not obligated to listen to him.\" But when there is no imminent and real danger, the Birkei Yosef wrote (YD 240:15): \"If his father was ill and tells him to give him to drink something that harms him, but certainly there is no danger, he should listen to him. And so it is implied from what the Sefer Ḥasidim wrote, siman 234, that in a matter of danger he should not listen to him, and it is evident that if there is no danger, even though it harms him, it is permitted.\" And with cigarettes the danger is not imminent and real, as I have already written in the note in Peninei Halakha, Likkutim II 9:14, note 8 (where this halakha was brought)."}]},{"slug":"11-01-21","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-21/","title_he":"כא – באילו נושאים חייבים לשמוע בקול ההורים?","title_en":"21. In Which Matters Must One Listen to the Parents?","body_en":"<p>A branch of the mitzva of honouring parents is not to contradict their words, that is, not to say to one's father or mother in a combative way, \"You are wrong!\" But one may discuss with them in a respectful way.</p>\n<p>And likewise it is a mitzva to listen to them and fulfil their wishes, but this is not an obligation; and if listening to them conflicts with something important to the son, he is not obligated to listen to them. For example: when the mother asks her grown son to wear a sweater on a hot day, out of exaggerated worry lest he catch cold… the son is not obligated to listen to her. Admittedly, if he wears the sweater he fulfils a mitzva, in that he listened to her and gladdened her. But in practice he is not obligated to wear it, for the essence of the mitzva of honouring parents is to assist them in something from which they have personal benefit, such as dressing them or feeding them or bringing them medicines; but listening to them is not an obligation, and since it is very uncomfortable for him to wear a sweater on a hot day, he is not obligated to listen to her. Also from the standpoint of the mitzva of reverence of parents he is not obligated to listen to her, for the essence of reverence is not to harm their honour. Therefore in a matter that does not concern them directly, there is no obligation to listen to the parents.</p>\n<p>However, a child who depends on his parents' table is obligated to listen to them, because they are responsible for him and know what is good for him. And also after the age of mitzvot, as long as the children live with their parents, they must take their requests into consideration, for it is not fitting to make use of them and not take their wishes into consideration. And certainly in everything connected with the arrangements of the home, they are obligated to listen to their parents.</p>\n\n<p>When the parents ask the son to fulfil a mitzva, he is obligated to fulfil the mitzva also from the standpoint of the mitzva of honouring parents. And when the son is under his parents' authority, even if they ask him to keep a custom that is not obligatory, he should make an effort to listen to them.</p>\n<p>If the son wanted to fast voluntary fasts and the parents ask him not to fast: if he has reached the age of bar mitzva, by law he is not obligated to listen to them, since the fast concerns him himself; but if he listens to them, he fulfils a mitzva. And since he has no obligation to fast, and all his desire is to sanctify himself and to do the will of his Father in Heaven, several poskim wrote that it is better for him to be meticulous in the mitzva of honouring parents than to fast voluntary fasts. But if the parents ask that he neglect some mitzva, or even a custom mentioned in the Shulḥan Arukh, he should not listen to them; for they too are commanded in the honour of Heaven, and one should not listen to them to neglect a mitzva or a custom (SA 240:16; Beit Leḥem Yehuda; above halakha 13).</p>\n<p>If the parents demand of their son that he study specifically a certain profession, such as medicine, whereas he wants to study computers: if he listens to them, he fulfils a mitzva. But he is not obligated to do so, for this is a decision connected with his life and not directly connected with matters of honour or reverence. And even if the parents claim that their very life depends on it, and that if he studies computers instead of medicine he will bring down their old age in sorrow to the grave, he is not obligated to listen to them, for this is not a matter directly connected to them. And if he is sure that it is better for him to study computers, because this profession suits him more and in it he will be able to succeed, it is preferable that he study computers. And he must present this to the parents respectfully and not insolently. And the parents must learn to recognise their children's independence, so that they do not err and raise an uproar around every independent decision of theirs.</p>\n<p>Especially when it concerns decisions connected with mitzvot and values – for example, the son wants to study Torah and the parents object, or the son wants to enlist in a combat unit and the parents object, or the son wants to settle in a pioneering settlement and the parents object, or the son wants to make aliya and the parents object – in all these cases the son must fulfil the mitzva, for whenever there is a conflict between a mitzva of the Torah and the parents' wish, the mitzva of the Torah comes first. And likewise regarding marriage, as explained in the next halakha.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"The basis of the distinction between matters that concern the parents' own persons and matters that concern the children is explained in the Mahari'k 166:3-4 regarding marriage, and the Rema brought it at the end of siman 240 in YD. And so it is explained in the Ḥiddushei Ha-Ramban on Yevamot 6a, and so wrote the Rashba and the Ritva there, and the Shita Mekubetzet, Bava Metzia 32a, and the Gra 240:36. And so wrote many Aḥaronim. (Many sources for this are brought in Yabia Omer part 8, YD 22:5.) And although there is no obligation to listen to them in a matter that does not concern their own persons, if he listens to them he fulfils a mitzva in giving them satisfaction. Therefore, when it concerns something that will cause the son distress, since there is no obligation in this he need not listen to them.<br>And likewise we find that the mitzva of honouring parents is not at the expense of the son's money; therefore, if he thinks that he will earn more by studying another profession, he may choose it. And even if the profession that the parents want him to study will cause him distress, the matter is considered like a financial loss, and the Mahari'k wrote similarly there. In connection with this, my teacher and master Rabbi Tzvi Yehuda Ha-Kohen Kook ztz\"l used to say that the parents must be educated not to think that their children are their property.<br>However, there are Aḥaronim who disagreed with the very basis of the Mahari'k, and hold that if not listening to the parents will cause them real distress, the son is obligated to listen to his parents even in a matter that does not concern them themselves. The Responsa Maharsham 1:101 brought them, and so it is implied by the Ḥazon Ish on Kiddushin, siman 148, 32a. According to their view, the whole permission not to listen to the parents regarding marriage or livelihood is because these things override honouring parents, and not as the Mahari'k and the other poskim wrote, that there is no obligation of honouring parents in matters not connected to the parents themselves.<br>In any case, in practice the parents' opposition is usually not so sharp, and then, according to all opinions, if it concerns an important decision that concerns the son's life, he is not obligated to listen to his parents. And when there is a harsh conflict between the parents' wish and the son's, and if the son decides against their view they will be very distressed, the halakha follows the Mahari'k, since most Rishonim hold like him; therefore the son is not obligated to listen to his parents in matters that concern his life. And in difficult situations it is right to consult a rabbi."}]},{"slug":"11-01-22","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-22/","title_he":"כב – הורים שמתנגדים לנישואים","title_en":"22. Parents Who Oppose a Marriage","body_en":"<p>Sometimes very difficult problems arise around the son's wedding. The son has chosen his partner, and thinks that her qualities suit his character and his aspirations, and so the young woman thinks too. But the parents oppose it, and in their view his choice is mistaken. Sometimes the parents are so forceful in their opposition that they threaten to break off ties, along with threats of ruinous curses that will fall on the heads of the young couple. And the question is asked: must the son listen to his parents and lose the choice of his heart, or should he do as he wishes while ignoring his parents' view? And likewise sometimes the case is the reverse: the parents oppose their daughter's decision, and the question is whether the daughter must listen to her parents.</p>\n<p>The halakha is that although the matter is very painful for the parents and concerns the future of their family, in the matter of marriage the son or daughter need not listen to their parents (Rema YD 240:25). Admittedly, the parents' intention is for the good, but every person has free choice to decide about his future and his life. It is true that sometimes family tragedies are caused by this, but there is no solution to it; the decision must be in the hands of the bride and groom. The son or daughter are not the property of the parents; they are independent human beings entitled to decide for themselves about their future. Of course, the son and daughter are obligated to listen to and consult with their parents, to know that presumably their intention is good, and many times they are right; but the decision must be that of the couple themselves.</p>\n<p>Many times, after the parents' attention is drawn to this halakha, the situation changes for the better, and instead of words of threats there is serious consultation. But if despite everything the parents oppose and announce a breaking of ties, even then the son or daughter need not set aside their view because of their parents' view. And as we have learned (in halakha 20), even if the parents demand of the siblings that they not take part in the wedding, they should not listen to them, for one does not listen to parents against the mitzvot of the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-23","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-23/","title_he":"כג – האם ליצור קשר עם אב גרוש בניגוד לרצון האם?","title_en":"23. Should One Make Contact with a Divorced Father Against the Mother's Wishes?","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> I am the son of divorced parents. I was three years old when they divorced. All my days I grew up with my mother, and only rarely did I meet my father. My mother raised me with devotion and love, but she planted in me hostility towards my father and tried to cut me off from him. Usually I would hear from her that he was putting off the payments he had to pay her for me, and that he did not care about me. Today I am in my thirties; for long years I have not heard anything from him. Even when he would call me once every few years, the conversations were short, so that I do not know whether he wants contact with me. I am uncertain whether it is advisable that I make contact with him, when it is clear to me that if my mother knows I made contact with him, she will be very hurt.</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> You are obligated to make contact with your father. For the mitzva of honouring parents requires a respectful attitude, and cutting off contact is a total lack of respect and utterly contrary to the mitzva of honouring parents. Admittedly, in exceptional cases in which contact always leads to severe quarrels, there is room to create a certain distance from the parents so as not to stumble in the severe prohibition of directly hurting them. But even then it is not a matter of total cutting off. And when there is no certainty that contact will create confrontations with the father, one is obligated to try to make contact with him.</p>\n<p>And one should not claim that perhaps the father is not interested in contact with the son, and therefore the son is exempt from the mitzva. For only when the matter is clear beyond all doubt is there no need to try to make contact; but when there is a chance that the father is interested in contact, one is obligated to try to make contact with him. And who knows, perhaps the father is very interested in contact with his son, but because the son always kept away from him, he understood that he was not wanted, and out of fear of a harsh reaction he no longer tries to make contact. And perhaps he does not know how to make contact with the son. And who knows how many more years he will live; perhaps he will die and the son will remain frustrated all his life for having missed the opportunity to create a reasonable relationship with his father.</p>\n<p>Even when the mother explicitly asks her son not to contact the divorced father, the son may not listen to her. For we have learned that one must honour parents on condition that they do not command one to transgress one of the mitzvot; but if they ask one to transgress one of the mitzvot, one should not listen to them (SA YD 240:15). Therefore, even when the mother demands of her son that he not honour his father, he may not listen to her.</p>\n<p>But he should try to dispel her fears, to tell her that he greatly appreciates her devotion to him, that he knows that she raised him and invested her whole life for him, and that he will always be grateful to her for it; and that in the contact with the father there is no expression whatsoever of distancing himself from her, Heaven forbid.</p>\n<p>And if it can be estimated that the mother will not be persuaded by these words, the son may hide from her the contact with the father, as the Sages said that one may deviate from the truth for the sake of peace (Yevamot 65b). But in any case, even if she finds out and is not appeased, the son is obligated to make contact with the father and honour him, as it is said: \"Honour your father and your mother\" (Shemot 20:12; Devarim 5:16).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-24","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-24/","title_he":"כד – איסור הכאה והאם מותר לבן לתת זריקה לאביו","title_en":"24. The Prohibition of Striking, and Whether a Son May Give His Father an Injection","body_en":"<p>Sometimes we hear of shocking cases of sons who strike their parents. In order to explain how severe the matter is, one should mention again what we have already learned, that the Torah commanded not to strike a Jewish person (Ha-Ḥinukh 595). The Sages further added and said that whoever raises his hand to strike his fellow, even though he did not strike him, is called wicked (Sanhedrin 58b). And all this is said of one who strikes an ordinary person; but one who strikes his father or his mother and draws blood from them transgresses a far more severe prohibition, whose punishment is death, as it is said (Shemot 21:15): \"He who strikes his father or his mother shall surely be put to death.\" And if he did not draw blood from them, he transgresses the general prohibition of not striking a Jewish person, and in addition he has neglected two mitzvot, of honouring parents and reverence of parents (above, halakha 3).</p>\n<p>As we have learned (in halakha 15), so that a case should not, Heaven forbid, arise in which a person strikes his father, the Sages said that one should excommunicate a father who strikes his grown child, for there is a concern that the son will become angry and strike his father back, to the point that blood comes out of him, and it turns out that the father caused his son to transgress the severe prohibition (SA YD 240:20).<sup>12</sup></p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> May the son or daughter perform on their parents a medical treatment that may cause blood to come out, or, since there is a special prohibition against drawing blood from the parents, must they refrain from this, even if they are doctors?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> On the one hand, it is clear that if the children treat one of their parents, and in the course of it blood comes out of him, they are not punished for it, since they did it unintentionally, with his consent and for his benefit. But on the other hand, it is related in the Talmud about one of the great Amora'im (Rav) that when a thorn got stuck in his flesh, he did not let his son take it out, lest as a result of removing the thorn blood come out of him, and the son would be found unintentionally transgressing the prohibition of wounding his father (Sanhedrin 84b).</p>\n<p>Therefore the halakha was ruled that a son or daughter who are doctors may not operate on one of their parents, or take a blood test from him. And even if the parent asks that specifically his son treat him, because he trusts him, or because hiring another doctor will cost money, they should nevertheless hire another doctor. Only when there is no choice at all – for example, there is no other doctor available, or they have no money to finance the operation – may the son or daughter perform the operation on the father or the mother.</p>\n<p>And likewise a parent who needs to receive an injection every day should not ask this of his son or daughter, lest in the course of it they draw blood from him. And it is preferable that the spouse learn to give the injections. But in a case where no satisfactory solution is found, and there is a concern that there will be situations in which the parent will not receive the injection he needs, the son or daughter may inject the father or mother.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"According to the Arizal, one who wants to repent completely for having struck his father or mother, after he asks their forgiveness, should fast 26 consecutive fasts, and receive 26 lashes every day, and have certain intentions in mind; see Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Shofetim 22. Today many are accustomed to redeem the fasts with charity, and one who wants to repent for this should go to his rabbi to consult how to repent."},{"n":13,"en":"The Rishonim disagreed about this halakha. In the view of the Rif, the Rosh and the SA YD 241:3, a son may not perform medical treatment on his father, even when his father asks it of him. And in the view of the Rambam, the Ramban and the Rema, ideally it is preferable that another person treat them, and when it is hard to obtain another person, the son is permitted. In practice the Aḥaronim ruled an intermediate ruling: a son may not give his parents a treatment that may draw blood, and even if the matter involves expense and trouble, they should make an effort to obtain another doctor; but in a time of pressing need, when there is no other possibility, the son may treat his father. So ruled Rabbi Herzog and Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, as brought in the book Gesher Ha-Ḥayim part 2, ch. 1. And see the book Mora Horim U-Khvodam ch. 10, se'ifim 14-18, who wrote so in the name of Rabbi Ben Tziyon Abba Shaul. And some hold that there is no dispute at all between the SA and the Rema in this law, but both agree to the ruling brought above (Baḥ and Ben Ish Ḥai)."}]},{"slug":"11-01-25","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-25/","title_he":"כה – ילדים מאומצים כלפי הוריהם המאמצים","title_en":"25. Adopted Children Towards Their Adoptive Parents","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> Are adopted children obligated to honour their adoptive parents?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> The mitzva of honouring parents from the Torah applies to the parents who bore him (biological parents) and not to adoptive parents (Sota 49a). But from the standpoint of moral obligation, since the adoptive parents treated him as a son, he too must treat them as one treats ordinary parents. And likewise the Sages said (Megilla 13a): \"Whoever raises an orphan boy or girl in his home, Scripture regards him as if he had begotten him.\" And in a certain respect the moral obligation of the adopted child is greater. For it is the nature of the world that parents care for and raise their children; but when a couple take an orphaned or abandoned child and care for him and raise him, their piety is greater, and consequently the obligation to be grateful to them for it is greater. And likewise the Sages said (Ketubot 50a) that one who raises an orphan boy or girl in his home and marries them off is considered \"doing righteousness at all times\" (Tehillim 106:3). And it is further explained in the Midrash (Shemot Rabba 45:6) that the Holy One, blessed be He, has treasuries for giving reward to the righteous, and among them a special treasury for giving reward to those who raise orphans in their homes.</p>\n<p>If so, it is clear that the adopted son is obligated to treat them with reverence and honour as a son must treat his parents, and even more. And if they need help, the adopted son must help them as far as he is able. And if they are ill, he should accompany them and support them in everything needed, as children are obligated to do for their parents. And after their passing, it is a mitzva for their adopted sons to say Kaddish after them. However, if they need medical treatment that may draw blood, they may ask this of their adopted son, for the Torah decreed to be careful in this only regarding biological children.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-26","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-26/","title_he":"כו – ילדים מאומצים כלפי הוריהם הביולוגיים","title_en":"26. Adopted Children Towards Their Biological Parents","body_en":"<p><b>Question:</b> Is an adopted child obligated to honour his biological parents who gave him up for adoption?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> It is a mitzva for a son to honour his parents who brought him into the world. Admittedly, regarding wicked parents who commit transgressions out of spite, the poskim disagreed whether there is a mitzva to honour them (above, halakha 16). But parents who gave their son up for adoption are usually not considered wicked, but as people who committed transgressions by mistake and without knowledge. If so, as long as they are not defined as wicked, according to all opinions it is a mitzva to honour them. And the obligation of honour is to assist them when they need help; for example, if they were ill, he should support them in their illness.</p>\n<p>And the son should not say: but the parents did not care for me, so why should I honour them? For after all, in their merit he was born, and every person who relates positively to himself and to his life must be grateful to those who brought him into the world. And if he is not grateful to them, it turns out that he does not recognise the importance of his own existence. And the more he honours his parents, the more he will recognise his own worth; for out of honouring his roots his own dignity too will grow, and thereby he will be able to uncover his special talents. Beyond this, even one who does not understand the reason for the mitzva must fulfil it, for so Hashem commanded us in the Torah.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> Is it right that an adopted son find out who his biological parents are?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> In general, it is fitting that an adopted son know his biological parents and his family members, in order to fulfil the mitzva of honouring parents and the other mitzvot of brotherhood that a person has towards his relatives, and also because of the concern lest, by not knowing them, he come to marry his sister or his aunt.</p>\n<p>However, in that the parents agreed to give him up and gave him for adoption, they also gave up his honouring them; and the halakha is that parents who waived their honour – their honour is waived (SA YD 240:19), and their son has no obligation to search for them and honour them. Nevertheless, if he knows them and honours them, he fulfils a mitzva. And perhaps they gave him up in their hard hour, and afterwards regretted it, and now they long for contact with him; therefore it is good that he search for them. But if he does not search for them, he commits no prohibition.</p>\n<p>When there is a concern that the discovery and exposure may create a crisis in his soul, it is preferable that he not search for them. And one who is in doubt about this had better consult with a wise, experienced and God-fearing person who knows him well.</p>\n<p>And likewise, if the adoptive parents may be very distressed by this, it is preferable not to find out who the biological parents are, because the adopted son is obligated to be grateful to them and honour them as an ordinary son must honour his parents. And since the adopted son has no obligation to honour his biological parents who gave him up for adoption, from the standpoint of the mitzva it is preferable that he not distress his adoptive parents rather than search for his biological parents. And if he very much wants to find his biological parents against the will of his adoptive parents, it is fitting that he consult about this with a Torah scholar who knows them well or who is versed in these fields.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-27","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-27/","title_he":"כז – יחס הגר להוריו הביולוגיים","title_en":"27. The Convert's Attitude to His Biological Parents","body_en":"<p>A convert who has converted, since he has become a Jew, is like a newborn child; therefore, by the law of the mitzva of 'honouring parents', he is not obligated to honour his biological parents. But he may not disgrace them, and all the more so he may not strike them or curse them (SA YD 241:9). And from the standpoint of morality it is a mitzva for him to treat them with respect, so that he not be considered ungrateful, for they begot him and cared for him. Therefore, when they are ill, he must assist them. And if it would please them, it is fitting that he ask the gabbai to say a 'Mi She-berakh' for their recovery; and after their passing he should say 'Ashkava' and 'Kaddish Yatom' for the elevation of their souls (Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:60).</p>\n<p>Sometimes there is a problem: on the one hand the convert wants to behave properly towards his biological parents, and on the other hand he does not want his children to be drawn after their religion and nation. Therefore the convert must find the middle line: he should limit his visits to the measure at which he will not be considered ungrateful towards them. When righteous converts make aliya to the Land and their parents remain abroad, everyone understands that they cannot visit the parents often, and the problem hardly exists. But in special cases it is fitting to visit them; for example, if one of the convert's parents asks to see his grandchildren before his death, because of the respect that the convert must show him, if he has the financial means, it is fitting that he travel abroad with his children to visit him (based on Igrot Moshe YD 2:130).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-28","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-28/","title_he":"כח – סבא וסבתא","title_en":"28. Grandfather and Grandmother","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva for every person to honour his grandfather and grandmother, whether on his father's side or on his mother's side. For grandchildren are considered as children; and further, included in the honour of the father and the honour of the mother is that their parents be honoured. But the mitzva of honouring parents comes first and is more important than the mitzva of honouring grandfathers and grandmothers.</p>\n<p>In the view of most poskim, the main obligation towards the grandparents is to rise before them and relate to them with honour, but the grandchildren are not obligated to assist them in everything they need. For example, if the grandfather is ill and needs help with his eating and his clothing, although if they care for their grandfather they fulfil a great mitzva, nevertheless the grandchildren are not obligated to stop their work in order to care for him; rather, this obligation is on the children alone. And some say that the obligation of honouring grandfathers and grandmothers is exactly like honouring parents, only that regarding precedence the parents come before the grandparents.</p>\n<p>According to all opinions it is clear that if the grandfather needs financial help, and the grandson has the means to help him, he is obligated to assist him. And if he does not want to give of his money, the court can compel him to give charity to his grandfather.<sup>14</sup></p>\n<p>A grandson may give medical treatments to his grandfather, even though there is a concern that blood will come out through these treatments. For the special care against drawing blood applies to the parents alone, and not to the grandfathers and grandmothers (the Torah Li-Shmah 265 wrote that although it is permitted, one who is stringent will be blessed).</p>\n<p>Also naming a baby boy after a grandfather or great-grandfather, and a baby girl after a grandmother or great-grandmother, is included in the mitzva of honouring parents. Admittedly, this is not an obligation, but one who names after them fulfils a mitzva – both for those who are accustomed to give the name after their death and for those who are accustomed to give the name in their lifetime (see Sedei Ḥemed, section Kaf, rule 104; Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Shofetim 27). Sometimes, because of a passing wish to give a fashionable name, parents give up this mitzva or push the grandfather's name to be a second name; and it is reasonable that with the passing of years, when they grasp the importance of passing on tradition, they will regret it. And who knows, perhaps for many generations this name passed down in the family, and now because of them the chain has been broken. Nevertheless it must be emphasised that the exclusive right to choose the name belongs to the parents, and they are not obligated to fulfil this mitzva, and their own parents should not stir up a dispute about it.</p>\n<p>However, when it concerns a strange name that may cause him distress when he grows up, it is right to make it a second name. And presumably the grandfather or grandmother whose name has now become strange agree to this too, for they want the good of their grandchild.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"It is clear to all that a grandson must honour his grandfather and grandmother. The question is what the source of the obligation is and how binding it is. The basis of the halakha is brought in the Rema YD 240:24. And although the Mahari'k in root 30 seemingly wrote otherwise, it is clear that he too agrees that one must honour them, a kal va-ḥomer from the honour one must show any honoured person, and further, because of his parents, who are obligated to honour their parents, the grandson must honour their parents. The whole dispute is about the definitions of the obligation: according to the Rema, as a branch of the mitzva of honouring parents, whereas according to the Mahari'k, from the standpoint of the honour of an important and dear person. And likewise regarding the mother's parents: the mother's parents and the father's parents are equal and one is obligated to honour them, and so is the view of the Sefer Ḥaredim in the name of the Menorat Ha-Ma'or, and in his view honouring grandparents is from the Torah. And so is the view of the Birkei Yosef 18, and the Ben Ish Ḥai, Shofetim, second year 30, and others. (However, according to the Gra 240:34 the obligation applies only to the father's parents. And it is reasonable that he too agrees that one is obligated to honour the mother's parents by the law of a very important and dear person. And so wrote Responsa Shevut Yaakov part 2, 94: the father's parents he should honour like his parents, and the mother's parents with the honour of an important person. And see She'elat Yavetz part 2, 129, and Ḥatam Sofer YD 345, and Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 240:44, and Minḥat Elazar 3:33.)<br>If the grandfather needs charity, the grandson is compelled; so wrote the Shakh 240:23 in the name of the Baḥ. And although he wrote this according to the view of the Rema, it is clear that according to the Mahari'k too he will be compelled to do so, for one compels charity, and according to the order of precedence the grandfather comes before other poor people."}]},{"slug":"11-01-29","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-29/","title_he":"כט – אשת אביו, אחים גדולים, חמיו וחמותו","title_en":"29. One's Father's Wife, Older Siblings, and One's Father-in-Law and Mother-in-Law","body_en":"<p>A person is obligated to honour his father's wife even though she is not his mother, as it is said (Shemot 20:12): \"Honour <b>et</b> your father <b>ve-et</b> your mother,\" and the Sages learned that the word '<b>et</b>' comes to include their spouse: if the parents divorced or were widowed, and the father married another woman, his son is obligated to honour her, as it is said: \"<b>et</b> your father\" – also your father's wife. And likewise, if his mother married another husband, the son is obligated to honour him, as it is said: \"<b>ve-et</b> your mother\" – also your mother's husband.</p>\n<p>This law is very important. There have already been cases of widows around the age of thirty-five who found a successful partner, but following the bad behaviour of their adolescent children, the partner fled the house, and the marriage did not succeed. And so they behaved towards all the offers that came afterwards. Meanwhile many years passed, the children grew up and left home. Then they no longer interfered, but their widowed mother was already older, past the age at which she could give birth; offers hardly came to her any more, and she remained alone for decades through their fault. This is what the Torah commanded, \"<b>ve-et</b> your mother,\" to include the mother's spouse.</p>\n<p>The Sages further learned that the addition of the 've' in the words '<b>ve-</b>et your mother' comes to include that a person must honour also his older brother (Ketubot 103a). And it makes no difference whether it concerns an older brother on the father's side or on the mother's side.</p>\n<p>Some hold that specifically the firstborn brother must be honoured (Beit Leḥem Yehuda), but in the view of most poskim it is a mitzva that a person honour all the brothers and sisters older than he is (Birkei Yosef YD 240:17).</p>\n<p>And this obligation of honour is not like honouring parents; rather, it is a mitzva to relate to them in a respectful way, but there is no obligation to assist them in everything they need, and there is no prohibition against addressing them by their name and the like.</p>\n<p>From the standpoint of morality and proper conduct, it is right to honour also the uncles and aunts (Birkei Yosef 240:18, in the name of Rabbeinu Yona).</p>\n<p>A person is obligated in the honour of his father-in-law and mother-in-law (SA YD 240:24). But this honour is not like the honour of his father and mother, but like that of honoured elders (Baḥ and Shakh 22). By the essential law he may call them by their name, since the law of 'reverence' does not exist towards them. But he must address them in a respectful way as is customary, such as Grandpa so-and-so, or Rabbi so-and-so. And where calling them by their name is considered an affront, he should refrain from calling them by their name.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"11-01-30","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/11-01-30/","title_he":"ל – שלא יפלה אדם בין בניו בירושה","title_en":"30. That a Person Should Not Discriminate Between His Sons in Inheritance","body_en":"<p>It is right for a person not to discriminate against one of his sons in his inheritance, even if he is insolent towards him and does not honour him and is not careful in keeping the mitzvot (Bava Batra 133b). For even when the son himself is not good, it is possible that his grandson will be good. And if his father deprives him, the son will be distressed and will distance himself further from the family tradition, and the concern increases lest he not educate his sons properly. Shmuel the Amora instructed his student Rav Yehuda not to be present at a place where an inheritance is transferred, even from a bad son to a good son. And so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (ḤM 282:1). And in difficult cases it is right to consult a Torah scholar about how to behave.</p>\n<p>A father who discriminates between his children stirs up dispute among them and destroys his family. The deprived brother will accuse the favoured brothers that with their flattery they distanced him from their father in order to take his share of the inheritance, and will bear them a grudge for it all his days. His children too will distance themselves from the other members of the family, and the family will be torn apart. It is reasonable that the sons whom he wants to favour will also in the end feel distance towards him. They may be glad that they received a larger share of the inheritance, but towards the father they will feel estrangement. For the bond between children and their parents should be absolute and eternal, a bond that does not depend on anything. And if they see that their father's bond with them depends on honour or flattery towards him, they will not remember him as a good father, but as a person very sensitive to his honour, who even with his own offspring behaves pettily, takes revenge and bears grudges.</p>\n<p>However, one must distinguish between one who has stumbled in transgressions but in general is connected to the tradition, and one who has left the tradition completely. One who has remained connected to the tradition – it is fitting not to disinherit him, for regarding him there is a reasonable chance that he or his sons will return to the tradition of Israel. But one who has left the tradition completely, to the point that he appears about to assimilate among the gentiles, and the chance that his sons will return to the tradition of Israel is small – there is room to consider disinheriting him from all or part of the inheritance (see Igrot Moshe ḤM 2:50; Dinei Mamonot by Rabbi Batzri part 3, gate 5, 3:11; Pitḥei Ha-Ḥoshen part 9, 4:1).</p>\n<p>One may suggest that when it seems that one son does not intend to educate his children in Torah and mitzvot, the father should devote a considerable part of the inheritance to religious education for his grandchildren and great-grandchildren. Thereby encouragement will be given to all his children, without exception, to send their children to religious education, and one who sends his son to secular education will lose his share himself. And nevertheless it is worthwhile that part of the inheritance be divided among all equally, to express the absolute bond between the father and all his children.</p>\n<p><b>Question:</b> May one give a larger share in the inheritance to a son who teaches in a yeshiva, whose salary is relatively low and who has more children?</p>\n<p><b>Answer:</b> If the aim is to support a Torah scholar, and the brothers too agree to this wholeheartedly and will not come to jealousy, one may add to him in the inheritance. But if they will be jealous of him, as may usually happen, one may not favour him. And so we learned in the Torah that Yaakov Avinu valued Yosef more than his brothers and favoured him a little, but since the brothers did not recognise his extra virtue, they were jealous of him, and as a result a terrible rift was created in Yaakov's family (Bereshit 37). This is what the Sages said (Shabbat 10b): \"A person should never single out one son among the sons, for because of the weight of two sela of fine wool (the coat of many colours) which Yaakov gave Yosef more than his other sons, his brothers became jealous of him, and the matter rolled on until our forefathers went down to Egypt.\"</p>\n<p>However, while the parents are alive, they can help more the son who studies Torah, and can even make a Yissakhar and Zevulun agreement with him. This the brothers can perhaps understand, because there is no absolute declaration here that this son is preferable to them. But in the inheritance itself, in which the final and absolute attitude towards the children is expressed, one may not discriminate without the matter being agreed wholeheartedly by all.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
